  "worked_solution": "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global maximum of a function is the largest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global maximum.\n\n- A global maximum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global maximum. There can be zero, one, or many global maximum points.\n\nFor our function, the maximum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=10.$ Therefore, the global maximum is $10.$ The function reaches this value at both $(x,y)=(2,4)$ and $(x,y)=(-4,4).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14197172/assets/bf82159ccb39f6c07fa870abe04911847dcbb240bd1cf5e6be8a1216fa9cf327.png)",
